<Project>

    <!--
        Every sample project restores through a committed packages.lock.json, one per project beside
        its own file, exactly as the projects under src/ do.

        This file exists rather than the property being inherited because MSBuild takes the nearest
        Directory.Build.props walking up from a project and stops, so nothing under samples/ would
        ever reach src/Directory.Build.props. A file at the repository root would reach these
        projects but no longer reach those, for the same reason.

        Keeping the two separate suits the samples anyway. They are deliberately not part of the
        build under src/: each references published NuGet packages rather than the projects in this
        repository - see the comment in any sample's .csproj - and each has its own solution. The
        one property they share with src/ is duplicated here, which is a smaller cost than an import
        chain that would imply the two builds are one.

        What this changes day to day: bumping a sample's pinned SodaFlow version now means restoring
        that sample and committing the lock file it rewrites, not just editing the csproj.

        RestoreLockedMode is what makes that stick, and it is set on CI only, for the reasons spelled
        out in src/Directory.Build.props. It matters more here than there: bumping a pinned version
        is the entire maintenance story for these samples, so forgetting the lock file is not an
        edge case but the most likely mistake anyone will make in this directory.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
        <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(CI)' != ''">true</RestoreLockedMode>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        The same .NET Framework reference assembly handling src/Directory.Build.props carries, and
        for the same reason: the SDK adds this package itself when it cannot use an installed
        targeting pack - on Linux and macOS but not on Windows - so a graph that leaves it implicit
        is a different graph depending on where it was restored, and a lock file cannot describe
        both.

        This changes nothing today. No sample targets .NET Framework: the heads are net10.0-windows
        and net10.0 (net8.0-windows and net8.0 for Counter), the view models net10.0 (netstandard2.0
        for Counter), and the condition below is false for all of them, so no package is added and no
        lock file moves.

        It is here for the two ways that could stop being true, both of which are one small edit
        away and neither of which announces itself. A sample gaining a net472 head would restore
        differently on a Linux agent than on a Windows one; so would this workflow moving off
        windows-latest, which is only pinned there because the WPF heads need it. Either would fail
        the same NU1004 the projects under src/ failed, and finding that out from a red build is
        worse than carrying four inert lines.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <AutomaticallyUseReferenceAssemblyPackages>false</AutomaticallyUseReferenceAssemblyPackages>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFrameworkIdentifier)' == '.NETFramework'">
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies" Version="1.0.3" PrivateAssets="all"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
